const seoArticle={
 title:"How to Research a LoloBuy Order: A Practical Product, QC and Parcel Checklist",
 intro:"This guide is an independent research note for shoppers who use LoloBuy as part of a buying-agent workflow. It does not promise stock, price, delivery time or availability. Those details can change at listing, warehouse and destination level, so the useful habit is to check the current information at every step rather than rely on an old screenshot, a copied spreadsheet entry or a comment made for another country.",
 sections:[
 ["1. Start with the exact product, not a vague name","The most reliable starting point is a precise product page or a search phrase that includes the item type, version, colour and size. A broad term such as “black jacket” produces too many near-matches; it also makes it easy to compare the wrong option later. Record the product title exactly as it appears, save the selected variant, and keep the price and option in the same note. If there are several sellers or batches, treat them as separate choices rather than as interchangeable copies. This small discipline makes every later decision easier: you can ask about the right item, compare the right photos, and avoid building a parcel around an accidental variant."],
 ["2. Read the listing before acting on a link","A link is only a route to a listing. It is not evidence that an item is in stock, that the displayed size will fit, or that a colour in a promotional image is still selectable. Before continuing, check the chosen option, the unit used in the size chart, the visible product description and any seller-specific notes. Where a measurement is supplied, compare it with a garment or item you already own instead of assuming that a letter size means the same thing across brands. This is especially important for shoes, outerwear and accessories with multiple versions. A good research workflow separates what is shown on the product page from what you hope the item will be."],
 ["3. Treat quality-control images as a comparison tool","QC or warehouse images can be useful because they let you compare the received item with the option you intended to choose. They are not a guarantee of authenticity, condition, fit or long-term durability. Use them for concrete checks: colour, logo placement, material appearance, visible marks, basic measurements when supplied, and whether the photographed item matches the variant you recorded. Look at more than one image when possible and check that labels, size and colour do not contradict each other. If an image is unclear, write down the exact question you need answered. A vague request for “better quality” is hard to resolve; a focused question about a measurement, a mark or the selected colour is easier to evaluate."],
 ["4. Keep purchase status and parcel status separate","A common planning error is treating an item that has been ordered as if it were ready for international shipment. Those are different stages. An order may still need seller confirmation, domestic movement, receipt, checking or storage before it can become part of a parcel. Keep a simple list with three columns: the item and chosen option; its current order or warehouse status; and the action you need to take next. This makes it easier to spot a missing item before you submit a parcel. It also avoids unnecessary support requests caused by comparing two different stages of the workflow. Dates and status words should be read as current indicators, not fixed delivery promises."],
 ["5. Plan a parcel around restrictions, not assumptions","International shipping is shaped by destination rules, carrier rules, dimensions, weight and the goods inside a parcel. The cheapest-looking method is not automatically suitable for every destination or every combination of goods. Before submitting anything, review the available line descriptions for your destination and compare what they say about restrictions, insurance options, declared value choices and tracking. Do not copy a declaration or a route choice from a social post: that post may involve a different date, country or parcel content. If you are uncertain, reduce the decision to facts you can check now—where the parcel is going, what it contains, its measured weight and the carrier rules shown for that route."],
 ["6. Budget for the full decision, not only the listing price","The product price is only one part of a buying-agent order. Domestic movement, service fees, optional checks, packing choices, international delivery, taxes or destination charges can change the final amount. A clear budget therefore has a product subtotal and a separate parcel allowance. This does not require predicting an exact final bill; it means leaving room for variables you cannot know before the item is received and packed. When comparing two products, compare the likely complete decision: price, size or version risk, need for additional checks, and the effect on the parcel. The cheaper listing can be the more expensive choice if it creates repeated replacement or shipping problems."],
 ["7. Protect your evidence trail","Save the link, order reference, option, screenshots of the listing when relevant, warehouse images and shipping record in one place. This is not about collecting clutter. It gives you a factual trail if you need to re-check a choice, understand a charge or explain a discrepancy. Use filenames that make sense later, such as the item category, colour and date. Keep the original page URL rather than relying only on a short description. If an option or listing changes, your notes show what you selected at the time. Good evidence also improves your future searches: you can identify which descriptions, measurements and sellers were useful without presenting past outcomes as guarantees for the next order."],
 ["8. Use search terms that match a real decision","For an SEO search or a database search, combine the platform name with the decision you need to make. Examples include “LoloBuy size chart check”, “LoloBuy warehouse photos”, “LoloBuy parcel planning”, “LoloBuy shipping destination rules” and a specific product category such as “LoloBuy watches spreadsheet”. These phrases are more useful than generic claims like “best” or “safe” because they lead to information you can evaluate. Search intent matters: a shopper choosing a size needs measurements; a shopper preparing a parcel needs route and restriction information; a shopper comparing items needs an exact listing. Match the query to the next action, then save only the information that changes that action."],
 ["9. Be cautious with reviews and claims","Public comments can show what questions other shoppers asked, but they are not a substitute for the current listing, current policy or current route information. A review may be old, incomplete, tied to a different destination or focused on one unusual outcome. Treat anecdotal material as a prompt for research, not as a conclusion. The more useful question is: what can I verify today on the item, the selected option, the current parcel rules and the destination? This approach also prevents overconfident content. An independent guide should distinguish between a documented interface feature, a shopper’s report and an opinion. Combining those categories into one claim makes the guide less trustworthy and less useful."],
 ["10. A calmer final check before you continue","Before you move from research to a live database or order page, pause for a five-point check: Is this the exact item? Is the selected option recorded? Have I read the size or variant information? Do the available images match the selection? Have I reviewed the current parcel choices for my own destination? If the answer to any of these is no, the next step is research—not speed. This is the practical value of a LoloBuy checklist. It does not remove uncertainty, but it turns uncertainty into clear questions that can be checked. FindSpreadsheet is intended as the next place to search current categories and product terms; the final decision should still be based on the current product, warehouse and shipping information you can verify yourself."]
 ]
};
const faqItems=[
 ["Is this an official LoloBuy website?","No. This is an independent research preview. It does not process orders, represent LoloBuy, sellers or carriers, and it does not provide platform support."],
 ["What should I save before continuing with a product?","Save the exact product link, selected colour or version, size, current displayed price and a note on what you need to check next. This avoids comparing the wrong variant later."],
 ["Do warehouse or QC photos guarantee the product?","No. They can help you compare visible details with your selected option, but they do not guarantee authenticity, fit, condition, stock or delivery outcomes."],
 ["Can shipping cost and availability change?","Yes. Parcel options can depend on destination, weight, dimensions, parcel content and current carrier rules. Always review the current route information before submitting a parcel."],
 ["Should I use another shopper’s declaration or shipping choice?","No. A previous example may concern a different country, carrier, date or parcel. Use the rules and options currently shown for your own destination."],
 ["Where should I continue the product search?","Use FindSpreadsheet to search a current category, product term or brand. Treat the results as a research starting point and check the live listing before making a decision."]
];
